the first reports were dire with  emergency services saying more than 200  miners could be trapped underground in  an illegal abandoned gold shaft near  Johannesburg the rescue services had  audio contact with a small group of  around 30 who claimed there were many  more like them in another section of the  mine underneath them cranes and other  equipment were brought in to try to  leave a boulders out which were blocking  the exit water was lowered down and then  six hours after the emergency operation  started slowly one by one looking dazed  and dusty the miners climbed out they  told their rescuers they'd been attacked  by a rival group of illegal workers who  deliberately thrown concrete blocks in  the shaft to track them underground  but after eleven of them had surfaced  apparently none the worse for wear they  were immediately arrested and suddenly  the operation ground to a halt if there  are any others down there they appear to  have decided to take their chances  underground rather than face the law  this is an illegal operation obviously  people are it was going to happen to  them when they come out when they come  out but we have the eleven that came out  and well we think we have done enough we  will leave the place open if they want  to come out they will come out you do  not know whether they still more because  when we try to call there was no more  answers and then we suppose that 11  people that we had how discouraged the  community is not to engage in this type  of activity that is very dangerous for  themselves and for those that are  supposed to perform rescue as they are a  scrappy homemade rope ladder seems to  have been how they got down in the first  place a small security presence will  remain just in case others surface the  rescue services and the authorities will  be very much hoping that this whole  operation acts as some sort of deterrent  to any other people who are thinking  about doing illegal mining the risks are  so high and it is so dangerous these  people had an incredible escape  the reality is though it probably won't  deter others Alex Crawford Sky News in  Johannesburg
 

